<body>
	<h2 style="text-decoration: underline;">CGHMN Domain Overrides API</h2>

	<h3 id="introduction">Introduction</h3>
	<article>
		This HTTP endpoint allows you to fetch a list of all domains the CGHMN overrides.<br />
		<br />
		We override domains for a few reasons:
		<ul>
			<li>
				<b>"Dead" Domains:</b>
				Domains which have existed once upon a time but are no longer active, redirected towards
				the CGHMN to revive websites and services that were once accessible on those domains.<br />
				These should not collide with real-world/clear-net domains.
			</li>
			<li>
				<b>"Live" Domains:</b>
				Domains which still resolve and offer services on the clear-net, but are not compatible
				with most retro machines on the network or are used for different services nowadays.<br />
				These might break modern services or make them entirely inacccessible, but at the same
				time add or return some functionality to retro software on the network.
			</li>
			<li>
				<b>Custom TLDs:</b>
				These are Top-Level Domain assignments for custom TLDs members of the network host their
				own websites and services under. TLD assignments on the network should not collide with
				TLDs existing on the real-world/clear-net, but with the ever-changing landscape of TLDs
				out there, it's not impossible that a TLD assigned to a member of the network becomes
				are real TLD out on the clear-net later down the line.
			</li>
		</ul>
		If you don't mind modern services potentially breaking on your retro machines and want to experience
		the full CGHMN experience, you can simply set your DNS server on your CGHMN router or individual
		machines connected to the CGHMN to <tt>100.64.64.64</tt> and start browsing!<br />
		This is the default configuration if you used our
		<a href="https://firmware.cghmn.org" target="_blank">image builder</a> or configured an existing
		router using the setup script according to the
		<a href="https://wiki.cursedsilicon.net/wiki/How_to_Get_Connected" target="_blank">How To Get Connected</a>
		wiki entry.
	</article>

	<br />

	<h3 id="how-to">How-To Use</h3>
	<article>
		If you have a more complex setup which requires "split-DNS" however, where some domains are resolved
		by one DNS server and other domains by another, you might find this API useful.
		By periodically querying the <a href="/lists/filtered" target="_blank"><tt>/lists/filtered</tt></a>
		endpoint, you can get the most up-to-date domains the CGHMN overrides and integrate them with your
		local DNS resolver.<br />
		<a href="#dnsmasq-example"><small>(See below for a dnsmasq example)</small></a><br />
		<br />
		You can also apply certain filters as query parameters, if you would like to control which domains are
		being returned by the API. Multiple values can be specified per filter by separating them with a comma.
		To see possible values for each kind of filter (<tt>domains</tt>, <tt>categories</tt> or <tt>types</tt>),
		navigate to <a href="/lists/filtered" target="_blank"><tt>/lists/filtered</tt></a> without any query
		parameters.

		<h4 id="filters">Available filters</h4>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<b><tt>allowed_domains</tt></b><br />
				Set a list of domains which shall be included in the domain name list. Any domains not in this
				list are being filtered from the output list, regardless of other filters.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?allowed_domains=microsoft.com" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?allowed_domains=microsoft.com</tt>
				</a>, allow only microsoft.com and *.microsoft.com domains.
			</li>
			<li>
				<b><tt>disallowed_domains</tt></b><br />
				Set a list of domains which shall never be included in the output list.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?disallowed_domains=microsoft.com" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?disallowed_domains=microsoft.com</tt>
				</a>, never allow microsoft.com or *.microsoft.com domains.
			</li>
			<li>
				<b><tt>allowed_categories</tt></b><br />
				Set a list of categories which shall be included in the output list. Any categories not in this
				list are being filtered from the output list.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?allowed_categories=quakeworld,phantasy-star-online" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?allowed_categories=quakeworld,phantasy-star-online</tt>
				</a>, allow domains restoring QuakeWorld and Phantasy Star Online functionality but nothing else.
			</li>
			<li>
				<b><tt>disallowed_categories</tt></b><br />
				Set a list of categories which shall never be included in the output list.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?disallowed_categories=blackhole-adobe,macromedia" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?disallowed_categories=blackhole-adobe,macromedia</tt>
				</a>, allow all overrides except ones that "blackhole" (block) certain Adobe servers
				or redirect Macromedia domains to a local server on the CGHMN.
			</li>
			<li>
				<b><tt>allowed_types</tt></b><br />
				Set a list of override types which shall be included in the output list. Any types not in this
				list are being filtered from the output list.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?allowed_types=tld,dead" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?allowed_types=tld,dead</tt>
				</a>, allow overrides for "dead" domains and custom TLDs, but not "live" domains.
			</li>
			<li>
				<b><tt>disallowed_types</tt></b><br />
				Set a list of override types which shall never be included in the output list.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?disallowed_types=tld" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?disallowed_types=tld</tt>
				</a>, allow all overrides except custom TLDs.
			</li>
		</ul>
		<br />
		Note: Domains specified in the <tt>allowed_domains</tt> and <tt>disallowed_domains</tt> filters match for the
		specified domain itself <i>and all subdomains under it</i>. So if you specify
		<tt>?allowed_domains=example.org</tt>, not only will <tt>example.org</tt> itself be allowed to be
		overridden, but also for example <tt>www.example.org</tt>.

		<h4 id="output-format">Output format</h4>
		In addition to filters, there is also the <tt>as</tt> query parameter, which controls in what format the
		list is being rendered. Currently, the following values are valid output formats:
		<ul>
			<li>
				<b><tt>text</tt> (Default)</b><br />
				Basic text output, with details about each domain entry, using pounds (#) to denote comments.<br />
				Each domain is on its own line, without any other decoration.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?as=text" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?as=text</tt>
				</a>
			</li>
			<li>
				<b><tt>list</tt></b><br />
				Simple list output without any comments or other formatting.<br />
				Each domain is on its own line.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?as=list" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?as=list</tt>
				</a>
			</li>
			<li>
				<b><tt>dnsmasq</tt></b><br />
				Outputs the list in a dnsmasq compatible configuration file format.<br />
				Thus, it can directly be imported by dnsmasq using the <tt>conf-file = ...</tt> configuration
				parameter.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?as=dnsmasq" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?as=dnsmasq</tt>
				</a>
			</li>

			<li>
				<b><tt>dnsmasq-list</tt></b><br />
				Outputs the list in a dnsmasq compatible configuration file format without comments.<br />
				Thus, it can directly be imported by dnsmasq using the <tt>conf-file = ...</tt> configuration
				parameter.<br />
				Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?as=dnsmasq-list" target="_blank">
					<tt>/lists/filtered?as=dnsmasq-list</tt>
				</a>
			</li>
		</ul>
		<br />
		Note: If you use an output format that includes an upstream server, currently only <tt>dnsmasq</tt> compatible
		formats, you can override which IP address is baked into the file using the <tt>upstream</tt> query
		parameter.<br />
		Example: <a href="/lists/filtered?as=dnsmasq&upstream=100.64.11.19" target="_blank">
			<tt>/lists/filtered?as=dnsmasq&upstream=100.64.11.19</tt>
		</a>
	</article>

	<h3 id="dnsmasq-example">dnsmasq Example</h3>
	<article>
		Let's say you want to only forward custom TLDs to the CGHMN with your local dnsmasq instance, but ignore all
		other domain name overrides the network offers.<br />
		<br />
		First, download and store <a href="/update-cghmn-dns-list.sh">this shell script</a> somewhere sensible,
		e.g. <tt>/opt/update-cghmn-dns-list.sh</tt>.<br />
		Make sure you can execute it by running <tt>chmod +x /opt/update-cghmn-dns-list.sh</tt>.<br />
		<br />
		Then, you can add the following entry to your crontab:<br />
		<tt>*/5 * * * * /opt/update-cghmn-dns-list.sh /etc/dnsmasq-cghmn.conf 100.64.11.19
			allowed_types=tld</tt><br />
		<br />
		Last but not least, add this line to your dnsmasq configuration, likely at <tt>/etc/dnsmasq.conf</tt>:<br />
		<tt>conf-file = /etc/dnsmasq-cghmn.conf</tt><br />
		<br />
		That's pretty much it. Now, every five minutes, this API is queried for the most up-to-date list of domain
		name
		overrides, with your filters applied, and loaded into dnsmasq if anything has changed.
	</article>

	<h3 id="domain-overview">Domain Overview</h3>
	<article>
		Below is a list of all currently overridden domains (and their subdomains):<br />
		<br />

		<b>Live Domains:</b> <a href="/lists/filtered?allowed_types=live" target="_blank"><tt>live</tt></a>
		<ul>
			<?php foreach ($orderedEntries['live'] as $category => $entries) { ?>
				<li>
					Category
					<a href="/lists/filtered?allowed_types=live&allowed_categories=<?= $category ?>"
						target="_blank"><tt><?= $category ?></tt></a>:
					<ul>
						<?php foreach ($entries as $entry) { ?>
							<li><?= $entry->domain ?></li>
						<?php } ?>
					</ul>
				</li>
			<?php } ?>
		</ul>

		<b>Dead Domains:</b> <a href="/lists/filtered?allowed_types=dead" target="_blank"><tt>dead</tt></a>
		<ul>
			<?php foreach ($orderedEntries['dead'] as $category => $entries) { ?>
				<li>
					Category
					<a href="/lists/filtered?allowed_types=dead&allowed_categories=<?= $category ?>"
						target="_blank"><tt><?= $category ?></tt></a>:
					<ul>
						<?php foreach ($entries as $entry) { ?>
							<li><?= $entry->domain ?></li>
						<?php } ?>
					</ul>
				</li>
			<?php } ?>
		</ul>

		<b>Custom TLDs:</b> <a href="/lists/filtered?allowed_types=tld" target="_blank"><tt>tld</tt></a>
		<ul>
			<?php foreach ($orderedEntries['tld'] as $category => $entries)
				foreach ($entries as $entry) { ?>
					<li>
						<a href="/lists/filtered?allowed_types=tld&allowed_categories=<?= $category ?>" target="_blank">
							<tt><?= $category ?></tt>
						</a>
					</li>
				<?php } ?>
		</ul>
		<br />
		<small>Last updated: <?= $lastUpdateFormatted ?></small>
	</article>
